  "worked_solution": "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(r,s)\\,\\text{d}r+Q(r,s)\\,\\text{d}s=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202s}=\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202r}.\n$$\n\nFor the equation $(1-s{e}^{rs})\\,\\text{d}r+(-r{e}^{rs}+4s)\\,\\text{d}s=0$, we have  \n\n$$\nP(r,s)=1-s{e}^{rs},\\,Q(r,s)=-r{e}^{rs}+4s.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202s} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202s}(1-s{e}^{rs}) \\\\  & =-\\,({e}^{rs}+rs{e}^{rs}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202r} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202r}(-r{e}^{rs}+4s) \\\\  & =-\\,({e}^{rs}+rs{e}^{rs}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202s}=\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202r},\n$$\n\nthe exactness condition is satisfied. Therefore, the given differential equation $\\text{is exact}.$",
